---
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";

/**
 * The terms.
 *
 * Written to say what this service actually does rather than to be long: it
 * runs commands on machines other people control, on behalf of software they
 * point at it, and the sentences that matter are the ones about who is
 * responsible for that.
 */
---

<Legal
  title="Terms of service"
  description="The terms under which Exeora is provided."
  canonicalPath="/terms/"
  updated="6 August 2026"
>
  <p>
    Exeora is operated by Leynier Gutiérrez González, an individual based in Mexico. Using the
    service, the CLI or this site means accepting these terms. Questions about them go to <a
      href="mailto:hello@exeora.dev">hello@exeora.dev</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2>What the service does</h2>
  <p>
    Exeora connects an MCP client to a directory on a machine you control. The Exeora CLI, which you
    install and run yourself, opens an outbound connection to the gateway and executes the tool calls
    that arrive on it inside the project directory you registered.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A new project allows everything.</strong> Until you set a policy on it, any AI client you
    authorize against that project can run anything inside its directory, on that machine, as the
    user account running the CLI. The dashboard can restrict a project to reading only, or to a named
    list of commands, and can ask you to confirm every change before it runs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Confirmation reaches only clients that speak MCP 2026-07-28. On a client speaking the 2025
    protocol, a call that would need confirming is refused rather than run unconfirmed. Register only
    directories you are willing to let an agent change, and revoke a machine from the dashboard the
    moment you want it to stop.
  </p>

  <h2>Your account and your machines</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      You are responsible for what runs on your machines through Exeora, including anything an AI
      client you authorized causes to run.
    </li>
    <li>
      You are responsible for keeping your sign-in secure, and for the machines and directories you
      choose to register.
    </li>
    <li>
      You may only register machines and directories you are entitled to give access to. If a machine
      belongs to an employer or a client, that is their decision to make, not yours alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      You may delete your account at any time from the dashboard. It takes every machine, project,
      authorization and activity record with it, and cannot be undone.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What you may not do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Use the service to reach a machine or a directory you are not authorized to reach.</li>
    <li>
      Attempt to use one account to reach another's projects, machines, tokens or activity records.
    </li>
    <li>Use the service to break the law where you are, or where the machine is.</li>
    <li>
      Deliberately overload the gateway, or work around the rate limits it applies.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    An account doing any of these may be suspended without notice. Where the problem is a mistake
    rather than intent, expect to be told about it first.
  </p>

  <h2>Availability</h2>
  <p>
    The service is provided as it is, with no uptime commitment. It may change, be interrupted, or be
    withdrawn. Nothing here is offered as a guarantee that a tool call will succeed, that a machine
    will be reachable, or that a command will finish.
  </p>
  <p>
    The CLI is published as <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@exeora/cli">@exeora/cli</a> under
    the MIT licence, and its own licence text governs it. These terms cover the hosted service.
  </p>

  <h2>Liability</h2>
  <p>
    To the extent the law allows, Exeora is not liable for loss or damage arising from use of the
    service, including data loss, changes made to a repository by an AI client, commands run on a
    machine, or time spent undoing either. The service executes what it is asked to execute; deciding
    what to ask for is yours.
  </p>
  <p>Nothing here limits liability that cannot be limited by law.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    These terms may change. The date above says when they last did. A change that materially reduces
    what you can expect will be announced in the dashboard before it takes effect.
  </p>

  <h2>Governing law</h2>
  <p>
    These terms are governed by the laws of the United Mexican States, and any dispute arising from
    them falls to the competent courts of Mexico.
  </p>
</Legal>
